              "text": "When we refer to \"clean gold\" (more formally known as **fine gold** or **24-karat gold**), its highest standard purity is typically marked as **99.9%** (999 fineness) or **99.99%** (9999 fineness). \n\nIt is never marked as **100% pure** because achieving absolute, perfect purity is both scientifically and practically impossible. The reasons for this come down to physics, chemistry, economics, and the limits of measurement:\n\n### 1. The Thermodynamic Problem (Entropy)\nIn physics, the Second Law of Thermodynamics dictates that a closed system will always progress toward a state of disorder (entropy). In chemistry, this means that it is physically impossible to isolate a macroscopic amount of any substance with absolute 100% purity. \nA single gram of gold contains roughly $3 \\times 10^{21}$ atoms. At that scale, it is impossible to prevent at least a few billion atoms of other elements (like silver, copper, iron, or lead) from mixing into the gold lattice.\n\n### 2. Environmental and Equipment Contamination\nTo refine, mold, and stamp gold, it must be heated to its melting point ($1,064^\\circ\\text{C}$ or $1,948^\\circ\\text{F}$) and poured. During this process, the gold is exposed to the environment:\n* **The air:** Oxygen, nitrogen, and microscopic airborne dust particles can settle into the molten metal.\n* **The tools:** Cruibles, tongs, and molds are made of materials like graphite, clay, or steel. Traces of carbon, silica, or iron from these tools inevitably leach into the gold at high temperatures.\n\n### 3. The Limits of Refining Technology\nRefining gold is a multi-step chemical process. \n* First, raw gold undergoes the **Miller process**, which uses chlorine gas to blow out impurities, bringing the purity up to about **99.5%**. \n* To get it cleaner, refiners use the **Wohlwill process**, an electrochemical reaction that dissolves the gold and plates it onto a highly pure cathode, bringing the purity to **99.9% or 99.99%**. \n\nWhile it is possible to use specialized laboratory techniques to reach \"five nines\" (99.999%) or even \"six nines\" (99.9999%) of purity, doing so is incredibly slow and expensive. \n\n### 4. Exponentially Diminishing Returns\nIn gold refining, the \"last mile\" of purification is the most difficult and expensive. Removing the final 0.1% of impurities requires massive amounts of energy, specialized chemicals, and time. \nBecause a 99.9% pure gold bar and a hypothetical 100% pure gold bar would behave exactly the same way, look identical, and hold virtually the same market value, the astronomical cost of trying to remove those last few trace atoms simply makes no commercial sense.\n\n### 5. You Can\u2019t Prove 100% Purity\nEven if a laboratory somehow managed to create a piece of gold that was absolutely 100% pure, **they would have no way to prove it**. \nScientific testing instruments (like mass spectrometers or X-ray fluorescence analyzers) have limits of detection. They can only prove the *absence* of other elements down to a certain threshold (e.g., parts per million or parts per billion). Because scientists can never measure with infinite precision, claiming \"100% purity\" is legally and scientifically indefensible.\n\n### Summary\nBecause of these physical and chemical limits, **99.9% (or 99.99%) is globally accepted as the gold standard of absolute purity**. For all practical, financial, and chemical purposes, \"three nines\" gold is considered completely clean and pure.",
